Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.969 align:start position:0%
 
very pleased to see the transport  secretary Mark Harper joins us once  again in the studio lovely to see you  this morning  um please don't take this the question  the wrong way but but with with trains  not running across most of the country  as transport secretary do you accept  that you have failed in your job no I  don't I have two roles here one is to  keep the railway industry working but  also I've got to be fair to the taxpayer  there isn't a bottomless pit of money to  go into the rail industry and I think if  most people listening to your show look  at the offer that's on the table they'll  think it's very fair and reasonable it's  in line with what most people in the  private sector are getting we've seen  unite workers at Network rail have  accepted the offer the tssa are  recommending that offer be accepted by  their members and even with the rmts  very strong recommendation to their  members to not accept the offer to turn  it down to reject it out of hand we  still saw nearly 40 percent of rmt  members wanting to accept it so I think  the tide is turning on opinion about  whether these offers are reasonable or  not and therefore I hope the union will  look at it again call off the strikes  and accept what is a reasonable payoff  but just in terms of of the rmt from  their perspective it isn't just about P  it is about T's and C's did you or did  you not instruct the real delivery group  to insert Clauses into the the offer  document regarding driver only operation  trains without guards on the reform of  the rail industry has been on the table  from the very beginning and absolutely  can you just answer that question did  did you direct the railed Recovery Group  to Putin driver only operation into the  document to the offer document no no the  detailed negotiations between the  employers and the trade unions between  them the overall amount of money  available obviously is something that I  have to set so the detail is up to them  the driver only chains are not a new  thing they've been running since the  1980s and over half of them was opposed  by every single Union representing real  workers since if that is not a wrecking  amendment I don't know what it is well  over half of passengers that go on the  railways they're on trains where driver  only operation is in in place and it's  been accepted by um train drivers so  it's actually not I want a deal here but  that's why when I met the unions I made  sure there was an improved offer from  Network rail an improved offer and an  offer came from the train operating  companies that hadn't previously been on  the table so that's what Mick Lynch  asked me when I met him and that's what  I delivered it's a reasonable offer for  reform and pay and I think the union  should accept it you genuinely believed  that the rmt would accept an offer  containing driver only operations  something that they have consistently  approved I mean miklin said this we went  on Sunday to to have the conversation  fully expecting the blanks to be filled  in on P the real delivery group said we  can't do what we said department for  transport have made us put driver only  operation into this document and it  strikes me that it looks very much like  you're happy for these strikes to go  ahead you inserted something that you  knew the rmt could not and would not  accept no look quite the reverse I don't  want these strikes to take place at all  they're bad for passengers they're bad  for businesses like for example the  hospitality industry which is going to  suffer or is suffering as a result and  they're not good for the rail industry  going forward I want the strikes to be  resolved that's why I made sure there  was an improved offer on the table and I  think some of the trade unions have  accepted it you know you say about  people not accepting it that one of the  trade unions is recommending exceptions  unite workers except did it yesterday  and I think the rmt should think again  it's a reasonable offer on the table  reform needs to take place reform has  been on the table is he lying is he  lying when you said that he went in on  Sunday and the real delivery group were  instructed by the DFT to put in driver  on the operation as you're lying or not  I haven't been involved in the detailed  negotiations between the uh train  operating companies and the trade Unions  that's for them to deal with I I haven't  been doing that I made sure there was an  offer on the table which I think should  be accepted and reform of how the  railways operate you know seven day  working all of the other things that's  been on the table from the very  beginning I suppose I suppose at least  the rmt should be appreciative of the  fact that that you yourself are willing  to discuss pay with them in a way in  which and you know the nurses unions are  not being talked to about pay why does  Steve Barkley why does the health  secretary believe that he doesn't have  to address the reason that nurses are  going out on strike in negotiations with  nurses well first of all I don't think  there's a difference here I've been very  clear it's not my job to negotiate in  the same way the health sector is not  negotiating but meeting with trade  unions and listening to them I think is  very reasonable why is he Lots on repeat  that is the reason that nurses are going  out on strike they have a meeting he  doesn't discuss pay the nurses go out on  the street he's listening to their  concerns but on pay specifically you  know we've got for the Health Service an  independent pay review body that's made  a series of recommendations for people  who work in the health service including  nurses who we value absolutely we've  accepted all of their recommendations in  full the 19 pay rise that the nurses are  asking for isn't affordable I don't  think it's reasonable and it would take  money away from Frontline Healthcare you  don't have to stick to what the  independent peer review body suggests  you could add to it so when we have the  general secretary suggesting they're not  a single Brown Penny mall was offered  you could well have done so had you  desired to do so isn't that the case  well look I think we've got to also look  into account what is Affordable and  we've got to you know what they're  asking for would cost 10 billion pounds  that money would come away from  Frontline Healthcare so it's about  giving a fair pay rise and for example  we we value nurses so last year when  there was a pay freeze in the wider  public sector we made sure that nurses  got a pay rise they were the only people  to do so because we valued them we've  made a reasonable payoff for this year  which the pay review bodies have  recommended that gives a 1400 pound pay  rise to nurses which means for the  lowest paid nurses they're actually  getting a rise of 90 is it fear that  nurses starting salaries have risen less  quickly than an MP salary  and since 2010 MP salaries have risen by  28 you know and you will accept that  nurses pay has been reduced in real  terms by between 10 and 20 is that fair  well look we have accepted what the in  we have an independent pay review body  for people who works  you don't have to do what the period  would say well no but the pay review  bodies made recommendations about rides  in the past which we have accepted in  full which means the nurse will get a  1400 pound pay rise which is targeted  also at the lowest paid which gives them  a larger payroll than other people but  you've got to balance that against  whether it's widely affordable the most  important thing is that we get inflation  under control that's what's at the root  of a lot of these issues and that's why  we've got to balance rewarding  hard-working public servants but also  not having inflation busting pay Rises  it will embed inflation is it fair that  MPS wages have risen faster than  starting salaries for police teachers  and nurses since  as you know MP's pay is set by an  independent body we have nothing to do  with setting it at all you'll remember  that change was made back in 2010 so MPS  have nothing to do with setting their  pay was an independent body it's not fit  and they may recognize that independent  Peabody got it wrong well I'm gently  suggesting is that when it comes to  nurses pay perhaps they've got this  wrong well look they take evidence both  from the government but also from the  trade unions they look at Recruitment  and Retention they look at all of those  things and they make recommendations and  we've accepted them and I think most  people will think that what's been  offered is reasonable it's in line with  what people are getting in the private  sector I think most people listening to  visible think they've had a reasonable  payoff for and I hope they accept it we  will see what happens on that front just  one last question though Telegraph and  times have been reporting that there has  been a deal done with Albania which  could speed up the process of getting  people who should not be in this country  back to where they came from what can  you tell us about that well look I'm not  going to comment on specular relation in  the newspapers but look the Prime  Minister thinks that getting migration  under control is incredibly important  he's been working closely with the Home  Secretary on this and when there are  specific things to announce we'll  obviously announce them in due course  I'll take that Minister good to have you  on the show thanks for joining us

